CONCLUSION
Writing a good ¦, short or long, is a े job. There are no
magic formulas that 华 success other than those timeless elements of a
strong 初 to start from, э characters, a É story and a
valid theme. Approach your material in a fresh and น way, and don’t
be 〜 to rewrite, rewrite and rewrite again.
The best short films embrace complex, personal issues — themes feature
films ù to back away from. Often this is the source of their success.
Their plots don’t have to be complicated, but their characters do. The
characters’ ¬ and needs should be strong, specific, and especially clear
to drive the story. Try ™ your   a central problem to cope
with, as well as Фd sub-problems, and let the plot flow from her
ʨ.
Think of the plot as the story you tell to explain how a character changes
from an initial state to where we leave her at the end of the film. The focus
on character pushing plot, and not the other way around, is what
ʩ all good · and し. While other § arenas
may have lost their focus on character as the defining story element,
characters thrive in short films. They are why the art form exists — to
explore the ư and inner nuances of what a character ق he ¬,
and really needs — and then to make visual the reconciliation between
these ¬ and needs. Unlike features, your short film will be as complex,
ʪ, त and, ा, as Б as your lead character.
In this book, I’ve attempted to outline the basic requirements of a
successful short film, † them with those of a feature length film,
while ñ that both are based the same ↑ ง. If you
can write an effective short film, chances are you can write a good feature
length ¦, too. Good luck!

